\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Exterior symbols preserve vectors, not Clifford products}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The [Grassmann algebra](fdip-008F) and the earlier
[configuration Clifford algebra](fdip-001B) share an exterior
vector-space model. For a quadratic form #{q} on #{V^*},
the symbol map is a linear isomorphism
##{\operatorname{Cl}(V^*,q)\cong\Lambda(V^*).}
This is \citet{Proposition 2.5, pp. 32-33}{meinrenken2013clifford}
and the equivalence used in the
[transported exterior-basis coordinates](fcap-0003).
The formal declaration \leanref{CliffordAlgebra.equivExterior}
supplies a module equivalence over any commutative ring in which
#{2} is invertible; it does not identify the products.}

\p{For example, an exterior generator satisfies #{\xi^2=0},
while the corresponding Clifford generator satisfies
#{\iota_q(\xi)^2=q(\xi)1}. For #{q(\xi)\ne0} these
cannot be the same multiplication under a unit-preserving map.
The quadratic form remains in Clifford contraction terms.
The [associated graded algebra](fcap-000J) forgets those lower
terms, but the Clifford algebra itself does not. Therefore the
[Berezin functional](fdip-008F) and its determinant formulas
belong to the exterior product; the vector-space comparison alone
does not transfer them to a Clifford product.}
